  "Question": "If\n\n\\[ \\begin{gathered} \\dfrac{4^x}{2^{x+y}}=8,\\\\ \\dfrac{9^{x+y}}{3^{5y}}=243, \\end{gathered} \\]\n\n\\(x\\) and \\(y\\) are real numbers, then \\(xy\\) equals:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(1\\dfrac34\\)",
    "B": "\\(4\\)",
    "C": "\\(6\\)",
    "D": "\\(12\\)",
    "E": "\\(-4\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Solution": "Since \\(4=2^2\\) and \\(9=3^2\\), rewriting each equation with a single base will turn its exponent into a linear expression. All bases are positive, so the power laws apply to the real exponents, and neither denominator can be zero.\n\nThe equations become\n\\[\n2^{2x-(x+y)}=2^3,\n\\qquad\n3^{2(x+y)-5y}=3^5,\n\\]\nor\n\\[\n2^{x-y}=2^3,\n\\qquad\n3^{2x-3y}=3^5.\n\\]\nPowers with base \\(2\\) or \\(3\\) strictly increase as the exponent increases. Thus equal powers with either base must have equal exponents:\n\\[\nx-y=3,\n\\qquad\n2x-3y=5.\n\\]\nThe first equation gives \\(x=y+3\\). Substituting into the second gives\n\\[\n2(y+3)-3y=5,\n\\qquad\n6-y=5,\n\\]\nso \\(y=1\\) and \\(x=4\\). Therefore \\(xy=\\boxed{4}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{B}}\\).",
